The Ice Age had its own giants. [[@mammoths|Woolly mammoths]] and woolly rhinos grazed the [[@grasslands|cold grasslands]] of Europe, Asia and North America. [[@sabre-tooth|Sabre-toothed cats]], giant ground sloths and dire wolves lived in the Americas. Many of these animals died out as the ice retreated, and as human hunters spread.

People lived through it too. In Europe, Stone Age artists painted horses, bison and [[mammoth|mammoths]] on cave walls.

{{story:mammoths}}

Another way to remember the order is to split it in two. The first four, Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars, are small rocky worlds. Then comes a gap filled with the [[asteroidbelt|asteroid belt]]. Then the four giants: Jupiter and Saturn, huge balls of gas, and Uranus and Neptune, colder worlds rich in ice.

There is a pattern in the sizes too. Jupiter is the largest of all, so big that more than 1,300 Earths could fit inside it. Saturn, with its rings, is next. Uranus and Neptune are a similar size, like twins. The rocky planets look tiny next to them.

{{story:planets-size}}

Many songbirds are not born knowing their song. Young birds listen to adults, then practise. Their first attempts are rough, a little like a baby's babbling, and improve over weeks. Birds raised alone often sing a strange, simple version. Zebra finches, small birds studied in many labs, must hear an adult sing while they are young, or they never get their song right. Scientists study them to learn how children pick up speech.

Some birds borrow songs from others. The greater racket-tailed drongo of Indian forests copies the [[@animal-communication|calls of other birds]]. In the USA, the northern mockingbird can learn dozens of other species' songs and is the state bird of Texas and four other states. Parrots take copying even further, as you can read in [[@parrots-talk|how parrots talk]].

{{story:frogs-monsoon}}

Lion-tailed macaques live high in the [[canopy|canopy]] of evergreen forests, where they eat fruit, seeds, flowers, insects and sometimes small lizards. They live in troops of about 10 to 20, led by one or a few adult males.

Each morning they move through the treetops, calling to keep the group together. They are shy and quiet compared with other macaques, and they rarely come to the ground in good forest. A favourite food is the fruit of a tall rainforest tree called Cullenia, which only grows in old, undisturbed forest. Females usually give birth only every couple of years, so populations grow very slowly.

{{story:western-ghats}}

People have used falling water for over 2,000 years. Water wheels, a cousin of the [[@wheel-invented|wheel]] on a cart, ground grain, sawed wood and hammered iron. In 1882, the first [[hydroelectric|hydroelectric]] power station to light homes opened on the Fox River in Appleton, Wisconsin, USA.

India was quick to follow. A small plant at Sidrapong near Darjeeling began supplying electricity in 1897, and it is still working. In 1902, a hydroelectric station at Shivanasamudra, on the Kaveri River in Karnataka, began sending power to the Kolar Gold Fields almost 150 km away.

{{story:water-cycle}}

In January 2001, a huge earthquake struck Gujarat. According to Prajapati, a newspaper photo afterwards showed broken clay pots described as the "[[@how-fridges-cold|poor man's fridge]]". A potter from Morbi named Mansukhbhai Prajapati took that idea literally.

After years of experiments, he designed a cupboard-shaped refrigerator made almost entirely of clay. Water poured into a chamber at the top seeps down through the walls and evaporates, cooling the shelves inside. It keeps vegetables, fruit and milk fresher for longer, with no electricity. He named it Mitticool, from the Hindi word mitti, meaning soil or clay. India's National Innovation Foundation helped to bring his invention to wider attention.

{{story:pottery}}

In 1914, two scholars in Germany, Erich von Hornbostel and Curt Sachs, published a system for sorting every instrument on Earth. It has four main groups, and they line up closely with the Indian ones. Chordophones have strings. Aerophones use air. Membranophones have a stretched skin. Idiophones vibrate as a whole, like a xylophone bar or a pair of manjira.

Each group splits again and again, like branches on a tree, so every instrument gets a number code. Later, a fifth group was added for electrophones: instruments such as [[@electronic-music|synthesizers]], where the sound itself is made by [[@electricity-made|electricity]].

{{story:sound-travel}}

In *Mr. India* (1987), a kind man who looks after orphans finds a watch that makes him invisible. Children loved its villain, Mogambo, and its mix of comedy and adventure. In the Malayalam film *My Dear Kuttichathan* (1984), three children free a little genie from a magician. It was India's first 3D film, and audiences wore special glasses to watch it.

In *Makdee*, directed by Vishal Bhardwaj, a girl in a village outwits a "witch" who is said to turn children into animals. The same director later made *The Blue Umbrella*, based on a story by [[@ruskin-bond|Ruskin Bond]], about a girl in the Himalayas and the beautiful umbrella everyone in the village wants.

{{story:reading-brain}}

Many of Kolkata's Durga images are made in Kumartuli, a crowded neighbourhood of potters and sculptors in the north of the city. Work starts months before. Artists build frames of [[@bamboo|bamboo]] and straw, then cover them in layers of clay from the banks of the Hooghly river.

Once dry, the figures are painted, dressed in real cloth and decorated with jewellery. The eyes are painted last, in a ceremony called chokkhu daan, the "gift of eyes". Kumartuli images are sent across India and even abroad.

{{story:pottery}}

In November 1989, aged 16, Sachin made his Test debut against Pakistan in Karachi. Pakistan had some of the fastest bowlers in the world. In a later match in that series, a bouncer from Waqar Younis hit him on the nose. Bleeding, he refused to leave the field and kept batting.

His first Test century came in England in 1990. In 1992, at Perth in Australia, he made a hundred on one of the fastest, bounciest pitches in the world, aged 18.

{{story:mumbai}}

*Hum Log* (1984) followed a middle-class family in Delhi and their daily troubles. It was India's first soap opera, and it was made to raise questions about family size, [[@womens-rights|women's rights]] and education. At its peak, it is said to have drawn tens of millions of viewers and huge piles of letters.

*Buniyaad* (1986) told the story of a family through the [[@partition|Partition]] of 1947. Older viewers who had lived through it wrote in with their own memories. TV was becoming a place where the country talked to itself.

{{story:television-invented}}

The phone doesn't keep a photo of you to compare. It keeps a mathematical summary, a long list of numbers sometimes called a faceprint. A program trained on millions of faces has learned which features best tell people apart.

When you look at the phone again, it makes a new list and measures how close the two are. Close enough, and you're in. The data is kept in a locked-off part of the phone's chip, not sent to the company.

{{story:twins}}

Rajaraja I came to the throne in 985. He was a careful ruler who surveyed land for taxes and kept records on temple walls.

In 1010 he completed the [[@india-wonder-brihadeeswara|Brihadeeswarar temple]] in Thanjavur, also called the Big Temple. Its tower rises about 66 metres, as tall as a 20-storey building. The whole thing is built of granite, though there is little hard stone nearby, so huge blocks must have been brought from far away. [[inscription|Inscriptions]] list the temple's dancers, musicians, cooks and lamp-lighters by name. It is still a working temple, and part of a UNESCO [[@unesco-sites|World Heritage]] Site.

{{story:bronze-casting}}

For centuries, the main port in this area was Muziris, a little to the north, where Roman ships once came for pepper. According to tradition, a great flood in 1341 silted up Muziris and broke open a natural harbour at Kochi.

Whatever exactly happened, Kochi became the new trading hub. The local rulers, the Rajas of Kochi, welcomed traders and made money from taxes. Kerala's pepper was so valuable that it was sometimes called "black gold", and it drew ships along the [[@trade-routes|trade routes]] of the Indian Ocean.

{{story:trade-routes}}

Just outside the city lies Sarnath, where the [[@buddha|Buddha]] gave his first teaching after his enlightenment, about 2,500 years ago. The emperor [[@ashoka|Ashoka]] later built a pillar there. Its lion top is now the national emblem of India.

{{story:buddha}}

Jains honour Varanasi as the birthplace of Parshvanatha, one of their tirthankaras, or great teachers. The poet-saint Kabir lived here in the 1400s. He was raised by a family of Muslim weavers and wrote [[@mirabai-kabir|poems]] that both Hindus and Muslims still sing. Tulsidas, who retold the [[@ramayana|Ramayana]] in Awadhi, also lived and wrote in the city.

By the time of the Roman Empire, ships were sailing straight across the Arabian Sea using the [[monsoon|monsoon]] winds. They came to Muziris, a busy port on the Kerala coast, loaded with gold coins and went home with pepper. Hoards of Roman coins have been dug up in south India.

The Roman writer Pliny complained that India drained Rome of huge amounts of money every year for luxuries like pepper. A cookbook from Rome uses pepper in most of its recipes, even sweet ones. When the city of Rome was besieged in 408 CE, the attackers' demands reportedly included about 3,000 pounds of pepper.

{{story:silk-road}}

When milk heats up, whey proteins begin to unfold and stick to each other. At the surface, where water evaporates and leaves them behind, they join with casein and fat. A thin layer starts forming at around 70 to 80°C, well before boiling.

Stir the milk and you break the skin. Leave it, and it gets thicker and stronger. That is why milk on a quiet flame often looks calm right up to the moment it suddenly rises.

{{story:soap-bubbles-colours}}
